# Step 24: panels and live views. Its first pull request's rules, the
# reload of a running session's configuration through the core and the
# reads the panels make, broken one at a time, with the tests that must
# fail. Written with the code (docs/steps/24/answered.md, "The design,
# before building", point 1).

[break] taking-copies-nothing
rule: A session's configuration in force takes the keys named from the newer configuration.
file: crates/cineris-config/src/load.rs
in: taking
find: Some(value) => merge::insert(&mut values, key, value.clone()),
replace: Some(_) => {}
caught-by:
    cineris-config::it reloading::taking_some_keys_keeps_the_rest_as_they_were

[break] taking-takes-everything
rule: The keys not named keep the values in force: what waits does not come in with what applies at once.
file: crates/cineris-config/src/load.rs
in: taking
find: let mut values = self.values.clone();
replace: let mut values = newer.values.clone();
caught-by:
    cineris-config::it reloading::taking_some_keys_keeps_the_rest_as_they_were

[break] the-trust-store-is-not-watched
rule: The trust store's file is looked at with the configuration's, so trust taken away outside is seen by the next look.
file: crates/cineris-core/src/reload.rs
in: judge
find: .is_some_and(|watch| !watch.check().is_empty());
replace: .is_some_and(|watch| watch.check().is_empty() && false);
caught-by:
    cineris-core::it reload::trust_taken_away_from_outside_is_seen_by_the_next_look

[break] trust-is-not-judged-again
rule: A reload judges the project's trust again and loads with what it judged. (A new allow rule is also stopped by the load's own check of the pins, so the tests that catch this are the ones where the store changed.)
